      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the cleanup, Inna. Few fixes (dropping _count/_total from histogram/guage, renaming locks__count → state__locks) look good. A few points before this merges:\n\n1. clients__confirmed_count → clients__confirmed_clients\n\n\"clients\" appears twice here — after rename_metric_names() the scraped name becomes nfs_clients_confirmed_clients. I\u0027d suggest clients__confirmed instead, which is cleaner and consistent with the other metrics in this subsystem.\n\n2. Histogram pluralization (_latency → _latencies)\n\nPrometheus conventions use singular nouns for histogram base names (e.g., http_request_duration_seconds in the official docs). Each observation is a single measurement; the histogram type itself implies a distribution. Also, 6 out of 9 existing latency/duration histograms in this codebase already use singular — this change would make the minority convention dominant. I\u0027d prefer keeping the singular form, and if possible, renaming the few existing plural outliers to singular as well (e.g., monitoring__scraping_latencies → monitoring__scraping_latency) so we have a consistent convention across the board.\n\n3. Counter suffix: _total → _count\n\nThe commit message states \"In Prometheus, _total is for counters\" — but then the patch removes _total from counters and replaces it with _count. _total is the canonical Prometheus counter suffix (OpenMetrics mandates it), while _count is what Prometheus auto-appends to histograms/summaries — so using _count on counters creates ambiguity. The existing _total on these idmapping counters is correct. For idmapping__max_groups_exceeded, adding a suffix makes sense, but it should be _total not _count.",
